UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'MY PRIOR WAS WRONG BY THE WIDEST MARGIN IN THIS BATCH AND THE RECORD SHOULD SAY SO PLAINLY. I predicted 0 relevant references; 23 of 46 match the screen, and the ramp-slope seam this project has hunted since batch 08 sits inside them. Batch 15 ranked this anchor LOWEST of its three deferrals and REF-00984 HIGHEST; the ranking was exactly inverted. The reason was derivable beforehand and neither of us derived it: a biomechanics paper measuring propulsion effort cites the propulsion-on-slopes literature, whereas a systematic review of skills TESTS cites the instrument-validation literature. Three admitted: Sanford Story and Jones 1997 (entry 8), Chow et al. 2009 (entry 40), and Vredenburgh et al. 2009, the last reached via M1 and resolved by title because its deposit carries no DOI. Staged not admitted: Gagnon et al. 2014 pushrim-versus-slope and the 1989 portable-ramps appraisal. DEPTH-1 IS ENFORCED -- none of the three is mined in this pass. || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26 5.2d): mined_ref_id derived -- this row''s own query_text names exactly one REF id that exists in evidence_sources. || RC1/RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26): result-payload link(s) derived from this search''s own query_text against the retrieval log, per section 2.1''s rule. (1 artefact(s)) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: mined_ref_id set to ''REF-00985'' || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: result artefact(s) added: retrieval-log/session_2026-09-18-research-batch-16-backward-mining-t2-anchors/4062c068e5f7280f.json', "mined_ref_id" = 'REF-00985' WHERE "exec_id" = 70;
UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'THE PRIOR HELD AND GAP-016 IS NOT CLOSED. Seven routes, every one persisted as an artefact because a failed retrieval is retrieved evidence of a failed retrieval: academia.edu 403 (two different copies), researchgate 403, JSTOR 200 but the body is a bot interstitial not the article, SafetyLit 503 then an empty body, OpenAlex 429 (budget exhausted for this environment, resets midnight UTC). CROSSREF IS THE ONE INFORMATIVE NULL: a bibliographic title search returns five neighbouring papers and NOT this one, which VERIFIES rather than assumes the no-DOI fact the whole GAP-016 problem rests on -- JAPR never deposited it. CONSENSUS returned the record with the same abstract and the byline Alison G. Vredenburgh et al., corroborating existence and authorship-lead at index level, still not full text. CONSEQUENCE, STATED PLAINLY: extraction 45 stays skim/preliminary, REF-01002 stays UNVERIFIED, determination gate 1 stays open, C10 keeps refusing the re-determination, and parameter 3 x MOB stays undetermined. Nothing about the 7 percent figure moved and nothing should have. AN UNRESOLVED METADATA DISCREPANCY IS ALSO RECORDED: TRID prints the fourth author as Weiner, Jerome M; a web-search summary rendered it Welner, J. M. Neither is a retrieved authoritative index and this batch could not settle it -- the stored author row says Weiner and is flagged as unconfirmed. || CORRECTED 2026-09-18: CORRECTED TWICE OVER, and the second correction was also wrong. This note says SafetyLit returned 503 then an empty body. The persisted artefact e3b0c44298fc1c14.txt is status=null, exit=35, bytes=0 -- a connect failure with no HTTP response -- so the ORDER is reversed. The session record then retracted the 503 outright, which is also false: the harness transcript records a separate WebFetch of the same URL at 21:17:17 returning HTTP 503 Service Unavailable, AFTER the connect failure. Both events happened, empty first. The 503 leaves no artefact because that retrieval went through WebFetch instead of retrieval_log.fetch, which is how a claim about a retrieval came to rest on nothing a later reader could check. Also: this row says seven routes; ten attempts were made and three were unpersisted. || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: RC1/RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26): result-payload link(s) derived from this search''s own query_text against the retrieval log, per section 2.1''s rule. (1 artefact(s)) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: result artefact(s) added: retrieval-log/session_2026-09-18-research-batch-17-vredenburgh-full-read-and-mining/32ed63159c77e21e.html' WHERE "exec_id" = 72;
UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'PREDICTION MET, AND THIS IS THE PREDICTOR FIRST PROSPECTIVE TEST RATHER THAN A THIRD RETROSPECTIVE OBSERVATION. Predicted >= 8 of the references would match; 19 OF 30 DO. The prediction was written into the deferral note in batch 16 and into this batch priors file, both committed before the pass ran, so it is a test and not a description. GAP-018 asked for three or four such passes before the predictor is treated as doctrine; this is one, and it should be counted as one rather than as confirmation. WHAT THE SEAM CONTAINS: the propulsion-on-slopes literature almost in full -- kinetic analysis on indoor and outdoor surfaces, uphill handrim biomechanics, load on the upper extremity, EMG during paraplegic propulsion, and TWO 1989 portable-ramp papers (10.1177/030802268905201209 and 10.3109/03790798909166390, the latter already staged as candidate 101 in batch 16, which is a cross-confirmation that the two anchors reference sets overlap where the predictor says they should). DEPTH-1: nothing discovered here is mined in this pass. || CORRECTED 2026-09-18: RETRACTED. This note opens PREDICTION MET, 19 OF 30. The 19 is a SCREEN ARTEFACT: the screen carried ''propuls'' and ''accessib'', so any wheelchair-propulsion paper matched regardless of slope, and REF-00979 IS a propulsion paper. Derive it rather than quoting either figure: python3 scripts/research/mining_screen.py --ref REF-00979 --compare. GAP-029 carries the retraction; GAP-018 and GAP-027 are superseded. The prediction was neither met nor missed -- it was scored on an instrument that could not measure it. || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26 5.2d): mined_ref_id derived -- this row''s own query_text names exactly one REF id that exists in evidence_sources. || RC1/RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26): result-payload link(s) derived from this search''s own query_text against the retrieval log, per section 2.1''s rule. (1 artefact(s)) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: mined_ref_id set to ''REF-00979'' || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: result artefact(s) added: retrieval-log/session_2026-09-18-research-batch-17-vredenburgh-full-read-and-mining/10fc55b4294e17eb.json', "mined_ref_id" = 'REF-00979' WHERE "exec_id" = 73;
UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'PRIOR SLIGHTLY LOW ON COUNT (5 of 25, predicted 0-2) AND EXACTLY RIGHT ON CHARACTER, WHICH IS THE MORE USEFUL HALF. Every one of the five is pre-1997 and NOT ONE CARRIES A DOI: a 1957 study to determine the specifications of wheelchair ramps; a 1971 architectural movement study whose Part 3 is titled Ramp gradients; a 1979 accessible-buildings study; a 1981 Dutch research paper on accessibility by means of ramps; and a 1993 review of technical requirements for ramps prepared as an attachment to a request for proposals. THIS IS THE FOUNDATION LAYER OF THE WHOLE 1:12 LINEAGE and it is invisible to every DOI-keyed pass this project can run -- the same blind spot that hid REF-01002 for six batches, now showing up as a systematic property of the pre-1990 accessibility literature rather than as one unlucky paper. It also means REF-01002 abstract claim that design recommendations rest on limited empirical research is CHECKABLE against a named set rather than taken on trust. DEPTH-1: nothing discovered here is mined in this pass. || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26 5.2d): mined_ref_id derived -- this row''s own query_text names exactly one REF id that exists in evidence_sources. || RC1/RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26): result-payload link(s) derived from this search''s own query_text against the retrieval log, per section 2.1''s rule. (1 artefact(s)) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: mined_ref_id set to ''REF-00980'' || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: result artefact(s) added: retrieval-log/session_2026-09-18-research-batch-17-vredenburgh-full-read-and-mining/0bb880bf0ac7b2c5.json', "mined_ref_id" = 'REF-00980' WHERE "exec_id" = 74;
UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'PREDICTION FAILED. Predicted >= 8; got 2 OF 23. The predictor said a paper cites the literature of its own independent variable, and ramp slope IS this paper independent variable -- yet its references are the TRANSIT-VEHICLE and ADA-guidelines literature, not the slope-biomechanics literature. The reason is visible once stated: a papers reference list follows its FIELD and its problem framing, not its independent variable. A transit-boarding study cites transit studies. That is a mechanism the predictor did not account for and it is fatal to the simple form of it. || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26 5.2d): mined_ref_id derived -- this row''s own query_text names exactly one REF id that exists in evidence_sources. || RC1/RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26): result-payload link(s) derived from this search''s own query_text against the retrieval log, per section 2.1''s rule. (1 artefact(s)) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: mined_ref_id set to ''REF-00983'' || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: result artefact(s) added: retrieval-log/session_2026-09-18-research-batch-17-vredenburgh-full-read-and-mining/20515281a437ca18.json', "mined_ref_id" = 'REF-00983' WHERE "exec_id" = 75;
