UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'NEAR-ZERO-YIELD, and the R14 diagnosis is WRONG INDEX rather than query shape or genuine absence. One hit, ED113892 (Bio-Engineering Services to the Developmentally Disabled Adolescent, 1975), which mentions wheelchair ramps only as an adaptive device in a school programme — off-slug. ERIC is an EDUCATION index: it held Steinfeld 1979 because that report was deposited there, not because ERIC covers built-environment research. SO ERIC IS A HIGH-PRECISION ROUTE TO A NAMED REPORT AND A POOR ROUTE FOR TOPICAL DISCOVERY IN THIS DOMAIN. Both halves are findings of this batch; neither was known before it, and the owner ruling recorded 2026-09-18 states the first half. || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: RC1/RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26): result-payload link(s) derived from this search''s own query_text against the retrieval log, per section 2.1''s rule. (1 artefact(s)) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: result artefact(s) added: retrieval-log/session_2026-09-18-research-batch-18-archive-route-ramp-threshold/4f0bb891df1d4816.json' WHERE "exec_id" = 80;
UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'RECORD CONFIRMED, FULL TEXT NOT OBTAINED — candidate 109 is now VERIFIED TO EXIST with full bibliographic detail, which it did not have when batch 17 staged it from a reference list. Open Library OL4653562M: "Four architectural movement studies for the wheelchair and ambulant disabled", by_statement "[studies directed by] Felix Walter.", published by the DISABLED LIVING FOUNDATION, 346 Kensington High St, London, 1971, 60 pp. NOTE THE AUTHOR DIVERGENCE AND ITS RESOLUTION: REF-00980 deposited reference list gives "Walter F."; the catalogue gives a corporate author with Walter as director. NEITHER IS WRONG and the batch prior named this exact risk as its dissenter clause — the reference list names the person, the catalogue names the issuing body. Recorded rather than silently picked. || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: RC1/RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26): result-payload link(s) derived from this search''s own query_text against the retrieval log, per section 2.1''s rule. (2 artefact(s)) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: result artefact(s) added: retrieval-log/session_2026-09-18-research-batch-18-archive-route-ramp-threshold/86115186f6e00a28.json, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-18-research-batch-18-archive-route-ramp-threshold/8b6b057edca66b56.json' WHERE "exec_id" = 83;
UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'ZERO-YIELD ON ALL THREE ROUTES, AND THIS IS THE PRIOR MOST DECISIVELY FALSIFIED. I put van der Voordt 1981 at the HIGHEST retrieval odds of the five (p~0.7 record, p~0.5 full text) on the reasoning that a named academic with an institutional repository is reachable. Open Library 0; Crossref bibliographic query returned 12.7M generic results with no match; the TU Delft repository search returned nothing parseable. THE REASONING WAS WRONG IN A WAY WORTH KEEPING: an institutional repository holds what the institution deposited, and a 1981 paper predates institutional deposit entirely. Author prominence today does not make a 1981 item reachable. Candidate 111 stays PENDING-VERIFICATION. || CORRECTED 2026-09-18: R5 MISCLASSIFICATION, CAUGHT BY THE GATE AND CORRECTED RATHER THAN WAIVED. This search was logged --target-evidence-type grey because van der Voordt 1981 has no DOI and I was working an archive route. THAT IS PRECISELY THE INFERENCE R5 FORBIDS: non-indexation in PubMed/Scopus is an INDEXING fact, not an evidence-quality fact, and a Dutch-language paper titled "Accessibility by means of ramps — Some research data from the Netherlands" describes itself as research data. Retargeted to clinical. THE ERROR IS WORTH KEEPING VISIBLE because it is the batch own thesis turned against it: this batch argued that DOI-keyed searching structurally hides the pre-1990 layer, and then used absence-of-DOI as a quality signal in the same breath. || CORRECTED 2026-09-18: target_evidence_type ''grey'' -> ''clinical'' (misclassification corrected; the replaced value is kept here because the column no longer holds it) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: RC1/RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26): result-payload link(s) derived from this search''s own query_text against the retrieval log, per section 2.1''s rule. (3 artefact(s)) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: result artefact(s) added: retrieval-log/session_2026-09-18-research-batch-18-archive-route-ramp-threshold/2737e91c55a25a7f.html, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-18-research-batch-18-archive-route-ramp-threshold/8edc7f5e3b84cb78.json, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-18-research-batch-18-archive-route-ramp-threshold/e203d43931b79a74.json' WHERE "exec_id" = 84;
UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'ZERO-YIELD, AND IT IS A PREDICTED RESULT RATHER THAN A FINDING. P4 put archive-route failure at p~0.8 and that is what happened. ERIC: 0. Internet Archive on the journal name: no JAPR content. GENUINE ABSENCE FROM THESE INDEXES, for the structural reason the prior gave — these archives hold public-domain and institutional material, and JAPR 2009 is neither. GAP-016 AND GAP-026 REMAIN OPEN AND UNMOVED. Batch 17 named what would actually work and it is still true: a library or document-delivery route (institutional access, ILL, or the corresponding author), which is not a search and cannot be run from this environment. TWO ROUTES FIRED, THEN STOPPED, EXACTLY AS PRE-COMMITTED — the point of the pre-commitment was to stop this batch re-running batch 17 seven routes and banking a second null as effort. || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: RC1/RC5 phase 2-data (DR-2026-09-26): result-payload link(s) derived from this search''s own query_text against the retrieval log, per section 2.1''s rule. (2 artefact(s)) || CORRECTED 2026-09-27: result artefact(s) added: retrieval-log/session_2026-09-18-research-batch-18-archive-route-ramp-threshold/d03a6fc8797e5f99.json, retrieval-log/session_2026-09-18-research-batch-18-archive-route-ramp-threshold/e0bc6cdc21012d2e.json' WHERE "exec_id" = 85;
UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'THREE OF FOUR PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTIONS FALSIFIED, AND THE REASONING BEHIND THE SURVIVING ONE WAS ALSO WRONG. Derive every figure here: python3 scripts/research/mining_screen.py --ref REF-01005 --compare. P1a FALSIFIED -- 38 references, not the 60-plus predicted. P1b FALSIFIED -- slope-strict scores 1-2, below the predicted 3-12 band. NOTE AGAINST MYSELF: my own pre-registered falsification condition said ''falsified if 0 or >=20'', which 1-2 does not trigger. The point prediction failed and the threshold I set to catch it was too lax to fire. Recording both rather than hiding behind the looser test. P1c FALSIFIED -- slope-loose scores 1-3 against slope-strict''s 1-2, nowhere near 3x. THE REASON I GAVE WAS WRONG IN A WAY WORTH KEEPING: I predicted the bare stem accessib would fire on everything because the report is an accessibility report. It fires on almost nothing, because the BIBLIOGRAPHY is not an accessibility bibliography -- it is a HUMAN FACTORS AND ERGONOMICS bibliography (Damon, Diffrient, Kroemer, McCormick, Murrell, Poulton, Human Factors and Applied Ergonomics as the recurring venues). A 1979 federal accessibility report drew its authority from ergonomics, not from a disability literature, and that is a finding about the period rather than about the screen. P1d CONFIRMED BUT WITH A DISCREPANCY THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE PREDICTION: both appear, and the Templer entry is 1974 on STAIR SHAPE, while Table 13 footnote c cites ''Templer, 1977''. Templer 1977 IS NOT IN THIS BIBLIOGRAPHY. THE YIELD IS A FLOOR, NOT A COUNT: the OCR is damaged, so an entry whose title is mangled below the screen''s terms is a false negative, and the band''s ceiling says how much could be hidden. TWO SLOPE-TITLED ENTRIES: Elmer C.D. (candidate 110, scores in the conservative band -- ''ramp'' is legible) and Corlett et al. ''Ramps or stairs'' in Applied Ergonomics 3 (ceiling only -- ''stairs'' is legible, ''Ramps'' is not, newly staged this pass). WALTER DOES NOT SCORE AT ALL, and that is the most instructive result: it is the single most slope-relevant item in the list, and its title in this bibliography carries no slope term because ''Part 3: Ramp gradients'' is a part title. A title screen cannot see it. Recorded so no later reader reads a low yield as a thin literature. || CORRECTED 2026-09-20: THE PREMISE OF THIS PASS WAS FALSE AND THE YIELD IT REPORTED WAS WRONG. Found by an adversarial pass over this same batch. This search recorded that the ED184280 scan was unreadable -- "no OCR engine is available in this environment (no tesseract, no pdftotext), so this text layer is the best that exists here" -- and transcribed 38 entries with authors marked illegible, years marked unresolvable and one slope term marked absent. THE PAGES ARE JBIG2 IMAGES INSIDE THE PDF AND THEY RENDER LEGIBLY IN ABOUT A SECOND. pypdf reads a TEXT LAYER; when that layer is mush it says the layer is mush and nothing whatever about the document. I read the mush as a property of the page and then reasoned at length, carefully, and wrongly about the consequences. WHAT THE PAGES ACTUALLY SAY, re-transcribed this batch from renders at 130 dpi and persisted as the superseding artefact c9361532746ffec9.json: Birren J.E. Psychology of aging 1964 (recorded as "C[u]rren" and filed at alphabetical position C, which then required a bogus note claiming Brattgard was out of order -- the list is in strict order and the misreading invented the anomaly); Corlett, Hutcheson, DeLugan and Rogozenski, RAMPS OR STAIRS, Applied ergonomics 3:4 pp 195-201 1972 (the word Ramps was called illegible); Elmer C.D. ... University of IL, 1957 (the year was called unresolvable); Jones J.C. Fitting for action (recorded as AUTHOR LOST TO THE SCAN); Kidwell A.M.; Kira A.; Nichols P.J.R.; Harrigan J.E.; Leonard J.A.; Steinfeld, EDWARD (the OCR read Eckard and I recorded Eckard as the reading); Templer J.A. ... New York: COLUMBIA UNIVERSITY 1974; Walter